<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#akkadian-language">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akkadian language</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Akkadian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Akkadian is an extinct Semitic language of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>. It became the administrative language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> and later of the Babylonian and Assyrian states. For centuries it served as the international language of diplomacy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>, used even in the correspondence of the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akkadian-language.jpg" alt="Akkadian language" width="320" height="359" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GilgameshTablet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Akkadian is an East Semitic language attested in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> from the mid-third millennium BCE. It is the earliest documented Semitic language and is named after the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, a major centre during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> (c. 2334–2154 BCE). Akkadian names appear in Sumerian texts from the mid-3rd millennium BCE, and texts fully in Akkadian begin to appear from about the 24th century BCE.</p><p>It was written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>, adopted from the Sumerians. Sargon of Akkad's empire introduced Akkadian as a written language by adapting Sumerian cuneiform. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a> it virtually displaced Sumerian, assumed to have been extinct as a living language by the 18th century BCE. By the 20th century BCE two forms were in use, Assyrian and Babylonian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Akkadian is a fusional language with grammatical case, and like other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semitic-languages">Semitic languages</a> it builds words on consonantal roots, most of them of three consonants. It has three cases and three numbers, though the dual is vestigial. Unlike most Semitic languages it uses subject–object–verb word order. Sumerian influenced its phonology, vocabulary and syntax, and scholars describe the two languages as a sprachbund.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">Cuneiform</a> suited it poorly, since it could not represent the glottal stop, the pharyngeals and the emphatic consonants. Scribes used signs as Sumerian logograms, Sumerian syllables or Akkadian syllables. The sign AN, for example, can stand for ilum ('god'), for the god Anu or for the syllable -an-.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In its Assyrian and Babylonian forms Akkadian was the native language of the Mesopotamian empires through the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> and became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> of much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Ancient Near East</a> by the time of the Bronze Age collapse c. 1150 BCE. It declined under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a>, by about the 8th century BCE, in favour of Old Aramaic. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> only scholars and priests in the temples used it, and the last known Akkadian cuneiform document dates from the 1st century CE.</p><p>Hundreds of thousands of texts and fragments survive, including religious narrative, legal and scientific works and letters. Literary works include the Atrahasis Epic, the Enûma Elish, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a>. The Chicago Assyrian Dictionary, in 21 volumes, was begun in 1921 and completed in 2011.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Akkadian language” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The cuneiform script, inherited from Sumer, was adapted to write the Semitic <b>Akkadian language</b> and eventually became the administrative script of much of the ancient Near East.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
